  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Sunday...\n\n'HOT' would be the perfect word to describe the overall forecast \nperiod. Dangerous heat stress continues to be the main concern \nstarting off the new work week and into next weekend. We went ahead \nand expanded the heat advisory to cover the rest of our CWA through \n9pm this evening. \n\nAfternoon guidance continues  to show a large upper ridge over our \nforecast area. This ridge will help suppress coverage of convection \nacross our CWA and keep weather conditions quiet across much of our \narea. With that in mind, there does remain some moisture which could \nlead to a few brief pop-up showers and storms across portions of \neast MS later this afternoon. A few lingering showers cannot be \nruled out across portions of the Golden Triangle later this evening. \nQuiet conditions will prevail across the area with overnight lows \ndipping into the mid 70s. \n\nDangerous heat will continue across the area looking ahead into \nMonday as the upper ridge remains locked in place over the southeast \nCONUS. Afternoon highs are forecasted to peak in the mid to upper \n90s. This combined with dewpoints in the 70s will cause heat index \nvalue readings to reach above 105 degrees across much of the area. \nFor this reason, the Heat Advisory has been expanded to cover the \nrest of our forecast area including portions of southeast AR and all \nof our northeast LA parishes. Furthermore, several areas along the \nDelta could see heat indices in excess of 110 degrees. A \n\"Significant\" risk for dangerous heat has been introduced for \nportions of the Delta. The heat advisory for Bolivar MS, Sunflower \nMS, Leflore MS, Washington MS, Humphreys MS, and Chicot AR has been \nupgraded to an extreme heat warning for Monday. The heat \nadvisory/heat warning will likely be extended into Tuesday and again \non Wednesday given increasing heat trends. There is a possibility \nthat the heat warning could be expanded further south heading into \nTuesday. \n\nWednesday will be the day for peak heating across much our \nforecast area. This is primarily due to compression and moisture \npooling ahead of a frontal boundary that will track southwards \nacross the area. This aforementioned feature will move into an \nunstable airmass, so a few strong to marginally isolated severe \nstorms cannot be ruled out mainly for areas east of I-55 Wednesday \nand Thursday. \n  \nA spoke of jet energy within northern stream flow looks to further \nweaken the ridge by late next week, which will leave the door open \nto some sort of organized, likely easterly, MCS. This will have to \nbe monitored in future updates. \n\nRegarding the tropics, the National Hurricane Center is currently \ntracking Tropical Depression Two as it continues to move north-\nnorthwest along the northern Gulf. The steering flow along the upper \nridge will redirect this system away from our area. Although no \ntropical impacts are expected, several areas along and south of Hwy \n84 could see locally heavy downpours with some storms as this system \ntracks along the Gulf. We will continue to monitor for any \ndeviations in the track. /CR/",
